 "transcript": {
  "answer": "**No. On the latest data, inflation is above target and has been rising.**\n\n**Inflation (ABS monthly CPI, year-on-year, August 2026)**\n- Headline CPI is **4.0%**. It was 1.9% in June 2025 and 3.5% in July 2026.\n- Trimmed mean, the RBA's preferred underlying measure, is **3.6%**. It has risen steadily from 2.8% in June 2025 and has been 3.6% for three months running (June to August 2026).\n- Both measures are above the RBA's 2–3% target band. Underlying inflation is not just a result of volatile items, because trimmed mean strips those out.\n- Headline CPI peaked at 4.6% in March 2026. It eased to 3.5% by July, then jumped back to 4.0% in August.\n\n**RBA response**\n- The RBA held the cash rate at about 3.6% from September 2025 to January 2026. It then raised rates, and the monthly average reached about **4.35%** by June 2026. It was 4.36% in September 2026.\n- That is a rise of roughly three-quarters of a percentage point. The rises began in February 2026.\n\n**Economy**\n- Unemployment has risen from 4.1% in January 2026 to **4.7%** in September 2026. The higher rates seem to be cooling the labour market.\n\n**Overall**\n- Underlying inflation has stopped rising for now, but it is about 0.6 percentage points above the top of the target band.\n- Headline inflation is still volatile and was back at 4.0% in August.\n- Rates have risen by about 0.75 percentage points and unemployment is climbing. The RBA is tightening policy and it isn't yet clear that this is bringing inflation down.\n\nI haven't checked which items drove the August jump. I can break it down by rents, electricity, fuel and food if you'd like.",
